1. What you may do

   - Read the source code.
   - Build the source code and run the result, for your own personal,
     non-commercial use, or to evaluate the app before deciding whether to use
     it.
   - Fork or clone the repository to study it, keeping this licence intact.

2. What you may not do

   - Redistribute the source code, or any binary you built from it, to anyone
     else — publicly or privately, modified or unmodified, free or paid.
   - Sell the software, or any modified version of it, or bundle it into a
     product or service that you sell or otherwise monetise.
   - Publish a derivative product based on this code — a fork positioned as
     its own app, a "clone", or a rebrand.
   - Remove or alter any copyright, licence, or attribution notice from the
     source code or from the app itself.
   - Use Nur Ifran Arpon's name, "Spaceadom", or any of the project's branding
     to imply endorsement of your own build or fork.

3. Official builds

   The only builds you should trust as "the real app" are the ones the owner
   publishes himself:

   - GitHub Releases on this repository
     (https://github.com/nur-arpon/Spaceadom/releases), and
   - the Microsoft Store listing, once published.

   A build from anywhere else — including one you compiled yourself — is not
   an official build, and distributing it as if it were one is exactly what
   Section 2 forbids.

4. Third-party components

   Spaceadom is built on open-source libraries (Rust crates, npm packages, and
   the Tauri framework, among others). Each of those keeps its own licence,
   unmodified by anything in this document. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md for the
   full list, including which of them require you to keep their own
   attribution if you build and redistribute anything containing them (most
   permissive licences, like MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD, do).

